## How the save record was merged
`COMMAND_BRIDGES` is assigned straight from `GENERATED_COMMAND_BRIDGES`, so the loop that builds bridge records runs *after* the old inline assignment and would have clobbered it. I therefore build **one** record after that loop, in the shape of the sibling plain-language bridges (`create:agent`, `create:manual-testing-playbook`): `id/name = command-speckit-save`, the inline record's plain variants (`save context`, `save memory`) plus the command's own marker (`/speckit:save`), a literal description (so a caller that empties `COMMAND_BRIDGES` still gets the record — two Python unit tests do exactly that), and `kind="skill"`. `kind` is the load-bearing field: with `kind="command"` the record loses the sort's `_kind_priority` tie to `system-spec-kit` on every "save context/save memory" prompt, which is the behaviour the retired inline record avoided. No owner-normalization entry for it, so it is not folded back to its owning skill.

